    "lem-qft-lattice-gauss-projection": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-qft-compact-lattice-gauge-data",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "In the joint basis define the real integer multiplier",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1",
            "3.1",
            "3.2",
            "4.1",
            "5.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "d-1.1",
          "claim": "The finite-support linear combinations of the joint basis vectors are dense in $\\mathcal H_{\\rm kin}$. On each joint basis vector, $G_x$ multiplies by the real number $\\gamma_x(\\mathbf n,\\mathbf m)$. The maximal diagonal operator is self-adjoint: if $\\phi\\in D(G_x^*)$ and $G_x^*\\phi=\\eta$, testing the adjoint identity against each basis vector gives $\\eta_{\\mathbf n,\\mathbf m}=\\gamma_x(\\mathbf n,\\mathbf m)\\phi_{\\mathbf n,\\mathbf m}$. Since $\\eta\\in\\mathcal H_{\\rm kin}$, the defining weighted square sum for $D(G_x)$ is finite, so $D(G_x^*)\\subseteq D(G_x)$; conversely Cauchy–Schwarz gives the adjoint identity for every $\\phi\\in D(G_x)$.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "given",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "d-2.1",
          "claim": "Every $G_x$ is diagonal in the same basis. Thus the spectral projections of any two $G_x$ are diagonal coordinate projections and commute, which proves strong commutation. On a basis vector, $U(\\alpha)$ multiplies by $e^{i\\sum_x\\alpha_x\\gamma_x}$; integer $\\gamma_x$ makes it $2\\pi$-periodic in each coordinate, and the diagonal phases give $U(\\alpha+\\beta)=U(\\alpha)U(\\beta)$ and $U(0)=I$.",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "step 1.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "d-3.1",
          "claim": "For finite linear combinations of basis vectors, $\\alpha\\mapsto U(\\alpha)\\psi$ is a finite sum of continuous characters. Such vectors are dense and all $U(\\alpha)$ have norm one, so for arbitrary $\\psi$ approximation by a finite combination proves norm continuity of $\\alpha\\mapsto U(\\alpha)\\psi$. This is strong continuity.",
          "step": "3.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "step 2.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "d-3.2",
          "claim": "From $[E_e,U_f]=\\delta_{ef}U_f$ and $[n_y,c_{x\\beta}]=-\\delta_{xy}c_{x\\beta}$ on the finite occupation core, one has $[G_x,U_e]=(\\mathbf1_{x=s(e)}-\\mathbf1_{x=t(e)})U_e$ and $[G_x,c_{y\\beta}]=\\delta_{xy}c_{y\\beta}$. Exponentiating these exact shifts on the joint basis yields the two displayed gauge-transformation laws.",
          "step": "3.2",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "step 2.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "d-4.1",
          "claim": "For every $\\psi\\in\\mathcal H_{\\rm kin}$, strong continuity from step 3.1 makes $\\alpha\\mapsto U(\\alpha)\\psi$ continuous on the compact torus. Its Hilbert-valued Riemann sums converge in norm: uniform continuity makes sums over partitions of mesh tending to zero a Cauchy family, and completeness supplies their limit. The normalized integral therefore exists, and its operator norm is at most one because each summand is an average of unitary images. For a basis vector with charge labels $\\gamma=(\\gamma_x)$, that integral is the vector itself if $\\gamma=0$ and zero otherwise, since $(2\\pi)^{-1}\\int_0^{2\\pi}e^{ik\\alpha}\\,d\\alpha=\\mathbf1_{k=0}$ for each integer $k$. By density of finite-support vectors and the norm bound, $P$ is the diagonal multiplier $\\mathbf1_{\\gamma=0}$ on all of $\\mathcal H_{\\rm kin}$; hence $P=P^*=P^2$ and its range is exactly the joint kernel of the $G_x$.",
          "step": "4.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "step 2.1",
            "step 3.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "d-5.1",
          "claim": "For each $x$, choose any $b_x$ of the $s$ fermion modes at that site to occupy. The occupation vector so obtained is normalized and has $n_x=b_x$; tensoring it with the zero-link Fourier basis vector gives every $\\gamma_x=0$. Thus it is a nonzero vector in $\\operatorname{ran}P=\\mathcal H_{\\rm phys}$.",
          "step": "5.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "step 4.1",
            "construct"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "routine_steps": [],
      "boundaries": [
        {
          "case": "empty",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "Statement inherits nonempty vertices/links; step 5.1 constructs a normalized physical vector for each allowed background, excluding an empty physical subspace. The potentially empty cycle list does not enter that construction."
        },
        {
          "case": "zero",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "step 4.1: the zero-charge character is exactly the range of P."
        },
        {
          "case": "one",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "step 5.1: the background occupation vector gives an explicit nonzero physical state."
        },
        {
          "case": "degenerate",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "Statement: the outgoing and incoming sums include a self-loop at both ends, where its contributions cancel; parallel links remain separately indexed."
        },
        {
          "case": "endpoints",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "step 2.1: integer Gauss labels make the action periodic at every 2π coordinate endpoint."
        },
        {
          "case": "nonempty-choice",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "step 5.1: each background value b_x is realized by occupying exactly b_x of the s modes."
        },
        {
          "case": "iff-forward",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "step 4.1: the diagonal Haar multiplier retains only joint zero labels."
        },
        {
          "case": "iff-reverse",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "step 4.1: every joint zero-label vector is fixed by the diagonal projection."
        }
      ]
    },
    "lem-qft-lattice-hamiltonian-selfadjoint": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-qft-compact-lattice-gauge-data",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "The operator $H_0",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1",
            "3.1",
            "5.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "d-1.1",
          "claim": "In the joint basis, $H_0$ multiplies coefficients by the real nonnegative number $h(\\mathbf n)=\\lambda_E\\sum_e n_e^2$. If $\\phi\\in D(H_0^*)$ with $H_0^*\\phi=\\eta$, testing against each basis vector gives $\\eta_{\\mathbf n,\\mathbf m}=h(\\mathbf n)\\phi_{\\mathbf n,\\mathbf m}$, so $\\eta\\in\\mathcal H_{\\rm kin}$ forces $\\sum h(\\mathbf n)^2|\\phi_{\\mathbf n,\\mathbf m}|^2<\\infty$ and $\\phi\\in D(H_0)$. Conversely that weighted square summability and Cauchy–Schwarz give the adjoint identity. Hence $H_0^*=H_0$. Truncating a vector to finite subsets of the joint basis converges both in norm and in the $H_0$ graph norm, so finite sums are a graph core.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "given",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "d-2.1",
          "claim": "Every $W_p$ is unitary, so $\\|W_p+W_p^\\dagger\\|\\le2$ and $W_p+W_p^\\dagger$ is self-adjoint. Each CAR operator has norm at most one by the conditional supplier in [F1]; every displayed fermion/link term is a finite sum of products of bounded operators, and pairing each term with its adjoint makes $V_F$ self-adjoint. Since the graph, plaquette list, site modes, and matrices are all finite-dimensional or finite in number, $V$ is bounded and self-adjoint.",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "step 1.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "d-3.1",
          "claim": "Let $\\beta>\\|V\\|$ and $z=\\pm i\\beta$. The diagonal resolvent $R_0(z)=(H_0-z)^{-1}$ satisfies $\\|R_0(z)\\|\\le1/\\beta$, and $$H-z=(I+VR_0(z))(H_0-z).$$ The Neumann series $\\sum_{k=0}^{\\infty}(-VR_0(z))^k$ converges in operator norm because $\\|VR_0(z)\\|<1$, so $I+VR_0(z)$ is onto and invertible. Thus both $H-i\\beta$ and $H+i\\beta$ are onto $\\mathcal H_{\\rm kin}$.",
          "step": "3.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "step 2.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "d-4.1",
          "claim": "On $D(H_0)$, $H$ is densely defined and symmetric because $H_0$ and $V$ are. To prove self-adjointness directly, take $y\\in D(H^*)$ and choose $x\\in D(H)$ with $(H+i\\beta)x=(H^*+i\\beta)y$, using the surjectivity from step 3.1. Then $y-x\\in\\ker(H^*+i\\beta)=\\operatorname{Ran}(H-i\\beta)^\\perp=\\{0\\}$, again by step 3.1. Therefore $D(H^*)\\subseteq D(H)$; symmetry gives the reverse inclusion and $H=H^*$. Its domain is exactly the original $D(H_0)$ because the sum with bounded $V$ was defined there.",
          "step": "4.1",
          "inputs": [
            "step 3.1",
            "step 2.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "d-5.1",
          "claim": "Each $U(\\alpha)$ is diagonal in the same Fourier/occupation basis as $H_0$, so it preserves $D(H_0)$ and commutes with $H_0$. The link relation $[E_e,U_e]=U_e$ and the CAR relation $[n_x,c_{y\\beta}]=-\\delta_{xy}c_{y\\beta}$ give $U(\\alpha)U_eU(\\alpha)^\\dagger=e^{i(\\alpha_{s(e)}-\\alpha_{t(e)})}U_e$ and $U(\\alpha)c_{x\\beta}U(\\alpha)^\\dagger=e^{i\\alpha_x}c_{x\\beta}$. A cycle's link phases telescope to $1$; in a hopping term the phases multiply to $e^{-i\\alpha_{s(e)}}e^{i(\\alpha_{s(e)}-\\alpha_{t(e)})}e^{i\\alpha_{t(e)}}=1$, and each onsite bilinear has phase $e^{-i\\alpha_x}e^{i\\alpha_x}=1$. Therefore $U(\\alpha)VU(\\alpha)^\\dagger=V$ and $U(\\alpha)HU(\\alpha)^\\dagger=H$ on $D(H_0)$.",
          "step": "5.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "step 2.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "routine_steps": [],
      "boundaries": [
        {
          "case": "empty",
          "status": "not_applicable",
          "reason": "The stated model requires a nonempty finite link set."
        },
        {
          "case": "zero",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "step 3.1: the Neumann bound remains strict when V=0 and the electric zero mode is retained"
        },
        {
          "case": "one",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "step 1.1: finite-support graph truncations cover the one-link case"
        },
        {
          "case": "degenerate",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "step 4.1: the range argument uses no simplicity or nondegeneracy of electric eigenvalues"
        },
        {
          "case": "endpoints",
          "status": "not_applicable",
          "reason": "This operator statement has no interval or endpoint parameter."
        },
        {
          "case": "nonempty-choice",
          "status": "not_applicable",
          "reason": "The bounded-perturbation proof uses no choice selection."
        },
        {
          "case": "iff-forward",
          "status": "not_applicable",
          "reason": "The statement is self-adjointness, not an equivalence."
        },
        {
          "case": "iff-reverse",
          "status": "not_applicable",
          "reason": "The statement is self-adjointness, not an equivalence."
        }
      ]
    },
    "lem-qft-lattice-physical-evolution": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "A1",
          "source": "def-axiom-of-choice",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Every family of nonempty sets has a choice function",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "4.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "lem-qft-lattice-gauss-projection",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "The normalized Haar average",
          "uses": [
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "lem-qft-lattice-hamiltonian-selfadjoint",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "$H$ commutes with every gauge transformation",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "thm-stone-one-parameter-unitary-groups",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "strongly continuous one-parameter unitary groups",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1",
            "2.2",
            "4.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "d-1.1",
          "claim": "Since $H$ is self-adjoint, $T=-H/\\hbar$ is self-adjoint. Stone's theorem [F3] gives $\\mathcal U(t)=e^{itT}=e^{-itH/\\hbar}$ as a strongly continuous unitary group with the stated derivative at $t=0$ and exact generator domain $D(H)$.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "A1",
            "F2",
            "F3",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "d-2.1",
          "claim": "For $x\\in D(H)$ and fixed $t$, the difference quotient at zero for $\\mathcal U(h)\\mathcal U(t)x=\\mathcal U(t)\\mathcal U(h)x$ equals $\\mathcal U(t)(\\mathcal U(h)x-x)/h$ and converges to $-i\\mathcal U(t)Hx/\\hbar$. The exact domain characterization in [F3] therefore gives $\\mathcal U(t)x\\in D(H)$ and $H\\mathcal U(t)x=\\mathcal U(t)Hx$.",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F3",
            "step 1.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "d-2.2",
          "claim": "For each $\\alpha$, the conjugated family $V_\\alpha(t)=U(\\alpha)\\mathcal U(t)U(\\alpha)^\\dagger$ is a strongly continuous unitary group. By [F2], its generator is $-iH/\\hbar$ on exactly $D(H)$, the same generator as $\\mathcal U(t)$. Uniqueness in Stone's theorem gives $V_\\alpha(t)=\\mathcal U(t)$ for every $t$.",
          "step": "2.2",
          "inputs": [
            "F2",
            "F3",
            "step 1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "d-3.1",
          "claim": "Averaging the commutation $U(\\alpha)\\mathcal U(t)=\\mathcal U(t)U(\\alpha)$ over normalized Haar measure gives $P\\mathcal U(t)=\\mathcal U(t)P$. Thus $\\mathcal U(t)$ and its inverse preserve $\\operatorname{ran}P=\\mathcal H_{\\rm phys}$, and the restricted group is unitary and strongly continuous on that closed Hilbert subspace.",
          "step": "3.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "step 2.2",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "d-4.1",
          "claim": "For $x\\in\\mathcal H_{\\rm phys}$, the restricted difference quotient is the same norm limit as in $\\mathcal H_{\\rm kin}$. It exists exactly when $x\\in D(H)$ by [F3]; therefore the restricted group generator has domain $D(H)\\cap\\mathcal H_{\\rm phys}$ and is $-iH/\\hbar$ there. Stone's theorem on $\\mathcal H_{\\rm phys}$ identifies its self-adjoint Stone operator as $-H|_{D(H)\\cap\\mathcal H_{\\rm phys}}/\\hbar$. Since multiplication by the nonzero real scalar $-\\hbar$ preserves self-adjointness, the restricted physical Hamiltonian is self-adjoint.",
          "step": "4.1",
          "inputs": [
            "A1",
            "F3",
            "step 3.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "routine_steps": [],
      "boundaries": [
        {
          "case": "empty",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "Statement uses the actual self-adjoint graph Hamiltonian and physical projection; step 3.1 restricts the group to their reducing range. The detected family is indexed by all real times and contains the identity at t=0."
        },
        {
          "case": "zero",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "step 1.1: the Stone group contains its identity at t=0"
        },
        {
          "case": "one",
          "status": "not_applicable",
          "reason": "No one-mode or one-site boundary case is asserted."
        },
        {
          "case": "degenerate",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "step 2.2: generator uniqueness does not assume a simple spectrum"
        },
        {
          "case": "endpoints",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "step 2.1: the domain argument applies to every real t, including positive, negative and zero times"
        },
        {
          "case": "nonempty-choice",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "step 1.1: Stone's theorem is applied under the explicitly stated Axiom of Choice"
        },
        {
          "case": "iff-forward",
          "status": "not_applicable",
          "reason": "The statement is an implication about invariant dynamics, not an iff."
        },
        {
          "case": "iff-reverse",
          "status": "not_applicable",
          "reason": "The statement is an implication about invariant dynamics, not an iff."
        }
      ]
    },
    "lem-qft-lattice-local-gauss-flux": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "lem-qft-lattice-gauss-projection",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "the $G_x$ strongly commute",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "d-1.1",
          "claim": "For every basis label $(\\mathbf n,\\mathbf m)$, summing $\\gamma_x(\\mathbf n,\\mathbf m)$ over $x\\in R$ cancels each link with both endpoints in $R$, leaves $+\\!n_e$ on a link directed out of $R$ and $-\\!n_e$ on a link directed into $R$, and subtracts $\\sum_{x\\in R}(m_x-b_x)$. Therefore $$\\sum_{x\\in R}\\gamma_x(\\mathbf n,\\mathbf m)=\\sum_{e\\in\\partial R}\\epsilon(e,R)n_e-\\sum_{x\\in R}(m_x-b_x).$$",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "given",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "d-2.1",
          "claim": "The equality of basis multipliers in step 1.1 is an equality of diagonal operators on vectors for which both sides are defined. Every $\\psi\\in\\mathcal D_R$ is in all $D(G_x)$ by physicality and in each boundary $D(E_e)$ by definition, while $N_R$ is bounded; applying the multiplier identity gives $\\sum_{x\\in R}G_x\\psi=(\\sum_{e\\in\\partial R}\\epsilon(e,R)E_e-N_R)\\psi$.",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "step 1.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "d-3.1",
          "claim": "If $\\psi\\in\\mathcal D_R\\subseteq\\mathcal H_{\\rm phys}$, then $G_x\\psi=0$ for every $x\\in R$. Substitution in step 2.1 gives the claimed regional charge/flux identity.",
          "step": "3.1",
          "inputs": [
            "step 2.1",
            "given",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "d-4.1",
          "claim": "For $R=V$, every link has both endpoints in $R$, so $\\partial R=\\varnothing$ and the flux sum is zero. Step 3.1 then yields $N_V\\psi=0$, which is exactly $\\sum_x n_x\\psi=(\\sum_x b_x)\\psi$ on all of $\\mathcal H_{\\rm phys}$.",
          "step": "4.1",
          "inputs": [
            "step 3.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "routine_steps": [],
      "boundaries": [
        {
          "case": "empty",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "step 4.1: R=V has empty boundary and gives the total background-charge constraint"
        },
        {
          "case": "zero",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "step 1.1: the multiplier identity includes zero Fourier flux labels"
        },
        {
          "case": "one",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "step 1.1: a singleton vertex region is covered by the arbitrary-subset sum"
        },
        {
          "case": "degenerate",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "step 1.1: internal links, including loops, cancel in the signed incidence sum"
        },
        {
          "case": "endpoints",
          "status": "not_applicable",
          "reason": "The graph identity has no interval endpoint parameter."
        },
        {
          "case": "nonempty-choice",
          "status": "not_applicable",
          "reason": "The finite incidence calculation uses no choice selection."
        },
        {
          "case": "iff-forward",
          "status": "not_applicable",
          "reason": "The claim is an identity, not an iff."
        },
        {
          "case": "iff-reverse",
          "status": "not_applicable",
          "reason": "The claim is an identity, not an iff."
        }
      ]
    },
    "lem-qft-lattice-ward-identities": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "A1",
          "source": "def-axiom-of-choice",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Every family of nonempty sets has a choice function",
          "uses": [
            "1.3"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "lem-qft-lattice-gauss-projection",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "the $G_x$ strongly commute",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.2",
            "1.3",
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "lem-qft-lattice-physical-evolution",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "It commutes with every gauge transformation $U(\\alpha)$ and with $P$",
          "uses": [
            "1.3",
            "3.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "d-1.1",
          "claim": "Physicality gives $U(\\alpha)\\psi=\\psi$ for every $\\alpha$. If $O$ has charge $q$, then $$\\langle\\psi,O\\psi\\rangle=\\langle U(\\alpha)\\psi,O\\,U(\\alpha)\\psi\\rangle=\\langle\\psi,U(\\alpha)^\\dagger O U(\\alpha)\\psi\\rangle=e^{-i\\sum_xq_x\\alpha_x}\\langle\\psi,O\\psi\\rangle.$$ If $q\\ne0$, choose a vertex $x$ with $q_x\\ne0$ and set $\\alpha_x=\\pi/q_x$, with all other components zero. The phase is $-1$, forcing $\\langle\\psi,O\\psi\\rangle=0$.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "given",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "d-1.2",
          "claim": "For $\\psi\\in\\mathcal H_{\\rm phys}\\subseteq D(G_x)$ and $O\\psi\\in D(G_x)$, self-adjointness of $G_x$ gives $$\\langle\\psi,[G_x,O]\\psi\\rangle=\\langle G_x\\psi,O\\psi\\rangle-\\langle\\psi,O G_x\\psi\\rangle=0.$$ If the commutator is initially defined on $D(G_x)$, its bounded extension has the same value on these vectors.",
          "step": "1.2",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "given",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "d-1.3",
          "claim": "Fix $g\\in\\mathbb Z^V$ and define the joint character projection $$Q_g=\\frac1{(2\\pi)^{|V|}}\\int_{\\mathbb T^V}e^{-i\\sum_xg_x\\alpha_x}U(\\alpha)\\,d\\alpha.$$ Since the joint basis diagonalizes the integer $G_x$, $Q_g$ is the orthogonal projection onto the joint eigenspace with eigenvalue vector $g$. The unitary $\\mathcal U(t)$ commutes with every $U(\\alpha)$ by [F2], hence with every $Q_g$. The exact domain is $$D(G_x)=\\left\\{\\phi:\\sum_{g\\in\\mathbb Z^V}g_x^2\\|Q_g\\phi\\|^2<\\infty\\right\\};$$ commutation and unitarity therefore show that $\\mathcal U(t)$ preserves $D(G_x)$ and commutes there with $G_x$.",
          "step": "1.3",
          "inputs": [
            "A1",
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "d-2.1",
          "claim": "A product $O_1\\cdots O_k$ of charge eigenoperators with charges $q^{(j)}$ transforms with charge $\\sum_jq^{(j)}$, by inserting $U^\\dagger U=I$ between adjacent factors. Applying step 1.1 to that product proves the finite-product selection rule.",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "step 1.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "d-3.1",
          "claim": "Gauge covariance of $O(t)$ follows from commuting $\\mathcal U(t)$ through the transformation law of $O$. Unitary conjugation preserves boundedness, and the domain relation in step 1.3 carries any $D(G_x)$ invariance and bounded commutator of $O$ to $O(t)$. The selection and infinitesimal identities already proved thus apply at every time.",
          "step": "3.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F2",
            "step 1.3",
            "step 1.1",
            "step 1.2"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "routine_steps": [],
      "boundaries": [
        {
          "case": "empty",
          "status": "not_applicable",
          "reason": "The result concerns a physical vector and a bounded operator."
        },
        {
          "case": "zero",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "step 1.1: the conclusion is conditional on q≠0; zero-charge expectations need not vanish"
        },
        {
          "case": "one",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "step 1.1: the character argument applies to one charged insertion"
        },
        {
          "case": "degenerate",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "step 1.1: any nonzero integer charge coordinate admits the phase π/q_x"
        },
        {
          "case": "endpoints",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "step 1.3: the joint-character projections give the exact G_x domains for the full real-time group."
        },
        {
          "case": "nonempty-choice",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "step 1.3: Stone evolution uses the Axiom of Choice and preserves every joint-character domain."
        },
        {
          "case": "iff-forward",
          "status": "not_applicable",
          "reason": "The selection rule is one-way and makes no converse claim."
        },
        {
          "case": "iff-reverse",
          "status": "not_applicable",
          "reason": "The selection rule is one-way and makes no converse claim."
        }
      ]
    },
    "lem-qft-qed-gauss-localization": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "lem-qft-lattice-local-gauss-flux",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "the region number $N_R=\\sum_{x\\in R}(n_x-b_xI)$ obeys the exact identity",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "d-1.1",
          "claim": "For every Fourier/occupation basis label, the sum of the Gauss multipliers over $x\\in R$ equals boundary flux minus regional number; this is the coefficient identity established in [F1]. Therefore the physical-domain identity $Q_R\\psi=\\sum_{e\\in\\partial R}\\epsilon(e,R)E_e\\psi$ holds.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "given"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "d-1.2",
          "claim": "For the continuum clause, $\\operatorname{supp}\\nabla\\chi_R$ is disjoint from $K$, so the assumed locality gives $[E(\\nabla\\chi_R),O]=0$ on $D$. The smeared Gauss identity therefore yields $Q_RO\\psi=OQ_R\\psi$ for every $\\psi\\in D$.",
          "step": "1.2",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "construct"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "d-2.1",
          "claim": "If $\\psi$ is physical and lies in each boundary $E_e$ domain, sector preservation makes $O\\psi$ physical, while commutation with each boundary $E_e$ makes $O\\psi$ lie in those same domains and gives $$Q_RO\\psi=\\sum_{e\\in\\partial R}\\epsilon(e,R)E_eO\\psi=O\\sum_{e\\in\\partial R}\\epsilon(e,R)E_e\\psi=OQ_R\\psi.$$ Thus $[Q_R,O]\\psi=0$. If also $[Q_R,O]\\psi=qO\\psi$ with $q\\ne0$, then $O\\psi=0$ for every physical $\\psi$ in the stated domain; this is incompatible with a nontrivial charge-changing action there.",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "step 1.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "d-3.1",
          "claim": "For $\\psi\\in D$, take the stated limits in step 1.2: $QO\\psi=\\lim_RQ_RO\\psi=\\lim_ROQ_R\\psi=OQ\\psi$, where the final equality uses boundedness of $O$ and $Q_R\\psi\\to Q\\psi$. Hence $[Q,O]=0$ on the dense domain. If also $[Q,O]=qO$ with $q\\ne0$, then $O\\psi=0$ on dense $D$; boundedness extends $O=0$ to the whole Hilbert space.",
          "step": "3.1",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "step 1.2",
            "algebra"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "routine_steps": [],
      "boundaries": [
        {
          "case": "empty",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "step 1.1: the finite identity includes the empty region as zero equals zero"
        },
        {
          "case": "zero",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "step 3.1: the continuum obstruction explicitly requires q≠0"
        },
        {
          "case": "one",
          "status": "not_applicable",
          "reason": "No one-dimensional continuum geometry is asserted; the continuum clause fixes three spatial dimensions."
        },
        {
          "case": "degenerate",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "step 1.1: all internal links cancel and the empty-boundary global case is retained"
        },
        {
          "case": "endpoints",
          "status": "not_applicable",
          "reason": "There is no time interval in the localization statement."
        },
        {
          "case": "nonempty-choice",
          "status": "not_applicable",
          "reason": "The finite sum and conditional limit argument use no choice selection."
        },
        {
          "case": "iff-forward",
          "status": "not_applicable",
          "reason": "The claim is an obstruction implication, not an iff."
        },
        {
          "case": "iff-reverse",
          "status": "not_applicable",
          "reason": "The claim is an obstruction implication, not an iff."
        }
      ]
    },
    "lem-qft-lattice-ward-contact": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "A1",
          "source": "def-axiom-of-choice",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Every family of nonempty sets has a choice function",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "lem-qft-lattice-ward-identities",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "time evolution preserves the gauge charge",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "lem-qft-lattice-physical-evolution",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "It commutes with every gauge transformation $U(\\alpha)$ and with $P$",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "d-1.1",
          "claim": "By the Axiom of Choice [A1], the self-adjoint $H$ generates the unitary group in [F2]. Its commutation with every gauge transformation implies commutation with the self-adjoint generators $G_x$ on $D(G_x)$; the domain-preserving statement is established in [F1]. Hence $G_x(t)=G_x$. Each $O_j(t_j)$ is bounded and preserves $D(G_x)$ by [F1], so every time-ordered product in the Statement is defined on $\\psi$.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "A1",
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "given"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "d-2.1",
          "claim": "Between two successive insertion times, the ordering of the fixed $O_j(t_j)$ and the single variable-time factor $G_x(t)$ does not change. Since $G_x(t)=G_x$, the ordered operator product and its expectation are constant on each such open interval.",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "step 1.1",
            "given"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "d-2.2",
          "claim": "When $t$ increases through $t_j$, the factor $G_x$ moves from the right of $O_j(t_j)$ to its left. The right-limit minus the left-limit of the ordered product is therefore the same ordered product with that factor replaced by $G_xO_j(t_j)-O_j(t_j)G_x=[G_x,O_j(t_j)]$; all other insertion orders remain fixed because the $t_i$ are distinct. The commutator is defined on the required domain and bounded by assumption.",
          "step": "2.2",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "step 1.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "d-3.1",
          "claim": "A piecewise constant locally integrable function has distributional derivative equal to the sum of its jumps times delta distributions: for every compactly supported smooth $\\varphi$, integration by parts on the intervals separated by $t_j$ gives $-\\int F(t)\\varphi'(t)\\,dt=\\sum_j(F(t_j+)-F(t_j-))\\varphi(t_j)$. Substituting the jumps from step 2.2 proves the displayed identity.",
          "step": "3.1",
          "inputs": [
            "step 2.1",
            "step 2.2",
            "algebra"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "routine_steps": [],
      "boundaries": [
        {
          "case": "empty",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "Statement explicitly assumes n>=1 insertions. At n=1 the product over i!=j in step 2.2 is the empty operator product I, giving the single contact term without deleting it."
        },
        {
          "case": "zero",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "step 2.1: away from all insertion times the distributional derivative is zero"
        },
        {
          "case": "one",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "step 2.2: for one insertion the sole jump is its commutator contact"
        },
        {
          "case": "degenerate",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "step 2.2: coincident insertion times are excluded by the pairwise-distinct hypothesis"
        },
        {
          "case": "endpoints",
          "status": "not_applicable",
          "reason": "The distribution is on the whole real time line with compactly supported tests."
        },
        {
          "case": "nonempty-choice",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "step 1.1: the time evolution premise uses AC through Stone's theorem"
        },
        {
          "case": "iff-forward",
          "status": "not_applicable",
          "reason": "The claim is a distributional identity, not an iff."
        },
        {
          "case": "iff-reverse",
          "status": "not_applicable",
          "reason": "The claim is a distributional identity, not an iff."
        }
      ]
    },
    "ex-qft-qed-nonempty-gauss": {
