    "lem-qft-finite-mode-occupation-basis": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "A1",
          "source": "def-countable-choice",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "> For every family $(X_n)_{n \\in \\mathbb{N}}$ of nonempty sets indexed by > $\\mathbb{N}$ there is a function $f$ with domain $\\mathbb{N}$ such that > $f(n) \\in X_n$ for every $n \\in \\mathbb{N}$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1",
            "3.1",
            "4.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-qft-fock-and-test-spaces",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "The bosonic and fermionic Fock Hilbert spaces are the square-summable direct sums",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "3.1",
            "4.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "def-qft-fock-and-test-spaces",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "fermionic creation by $c_n^\\dagger(h)\\Psi_n=\\sqrt{n+1}P_{a,n+1}(h\\otimes\\Psi_n)",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "lem-qft-fock-ccr-car",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "The fermionic $c(h)$ and $c^\\dagger(h)$ extend to bounded operators on the whole Fock space, with norms $\\|h\\|$, and satisfy on the whole space",
          "uses": [
            "3.1",
            "4.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "lem-nrqm-composites-hilbert-tensor-construction",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "then $(\\kappa(e_a\\otimes f_b))_{(a,b)\\in A\\times B}$ is an orthonormal family in $\\widehat{H\\odot K}$ whose closed linear span is $\\widehat{H\\odot K}$",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "lem-nrqm-composites-permutation-sector-projections",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "are orthogonal projections.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1",
            "3.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "d-1.1",
          "claim": "For $k=0$, $\\xi_\\varnothing=\\Omega$ is the unit vacuum. For $k\\ge1$, repeated application of the creation formula in [F2] gives $$\\xi_S=\\sqrt{k!}\\,P_{a,k}(e_{j_1}\\otimes\\cdots\\otimes e_{j_k}).$$ The product-basis theorem in [F4], under [A1], makes elementary tensors of basis vectors a complete orthonormal basis of $\\mathcal K^{\\widehat\\otimes k}$. If an index repeats, antisymmetrization is zero by pairing permutations that differ by swapping the equal factors. If all indices are distinct, their antisymmetrization is, up to the permutation sign, a scalar multiple of the vector displayed for the uniquely ordered subset $S$.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "A1",
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "F4",
            "given",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "d-2.1",
          "claim": "For $k\\ge1$ and distinct increasing indices, $\\|P_{a,k}(e_{j_1}\\otimes\\cdots\\otimes e_{j_k})\\|^2=\\langle e_{j_1}\\otimes\\cdots\\otimes e_{j_k},P_{a,k}(e_{j_1}\\otimes\\cdots\\otimes e_{j_k})\\rangle_2=1/k!$, since only the identity permutation has nonzero inner product with that ordered elementary tensor. Distinct subsets have orthogonal permutation orbits in the tensor basis, so $\\langle\\xi_S,\\xi_T\\rangle_2=\\mathbf1_{S=T}$. The vacuum case has norm one by step 1.1.",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "A1",
            "F4",
            "step 1.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "d-3.1",
          "claim": "For each $k\\ge1$, the bounded projection $P_{a,k}$ maps the complete basis of elementary tensors onto a dense subspace of its range. Step 1.1 shows this image is spanned by the $\\xi_S$ with $|S|=k$; step 2.1 shows those vectors are orthonormal. Thus they form a basis of each antisymmetric sector. If $k>|J|$, every elementary basis tensor repeats an index and its projection vanishes, so that sector is zero. The direct-sum construction and completeness supplied by [A1], [F1] and [F3], together with the unit vacuum in step 1.1, now show that all $\\xi_S$ form an orthonormal basis of $\\mathcal F_a(\\mathcal K)$.",
          "step": "3.1",
          "inputs": [
            "A1",
            "F1",
            "F3",
            "F4",
            "step 1.1",
